# IntelliJ IDEA Installation and Setup

This guide prepares IntelliJ IDEA for the programming lessons in this repository. It focuses on installing the IDE correctly and confirming that it opens successfully.

> This page covers the IDE installation only. Java Development Kit (JDK) configuration will be documented as the next separate lesson.

## What is IntelliJ IDEA?

IntelliJ IDEA is a cross-platform integrated development environment developed by JetBrains. It combines a code editor, project explorer, build integration, debugger, terminal, version-control tools, and extensibility through plugins.

It is commonly used for Java and Kotlin development.

## Current product model

JetBrains now presents IntelliJ IDEA as a unified product. Core Java and Kotlin development features are available at no cost, while advanced capabilities require an IntelliJ IDEA Ultimate subscription.

JetBrains also continues to provide access to the open-source Community Edition. For this learning repository, the free functionality is sufficient.

Use the official download page to select the appropriate installer:

- [Download IntelliJ IDEA](https://www.jetbrains.com/idea/download/)
- [JetBrains installation guide](https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/installation-guide.html)

Licensing and included features can change, so consult the official comparison shown by JetBrains before choosing a paid plan.

## Official system requirements

The current JetBrains installation documentation lists the following baseline requirements.

| Component | Requirement |
|---|---|
| CPU | x86_64 or ARM64 processor with four cores |
| Memory | 8 GB total RAM, with approximately 3 GB available to IDE processes |
| Storage | 10 GB of available disk space |
| Display | Minimum resolution of 1280 × 720 |
| Windows | Windows 10 or Windows 11 |
| macOS | A currently supported macOS version listed by JetBrains |
| Linux | A supported 64-bit distribution and desktop environment listed by JetBrains |

Check the [official installation guide](https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/installation-guide.html) before installing because supported operating-system versions change over time.

### Recommended learning environment

For a comfortable beginner experience:

- use 16 GB RAM when available;
- install the IDE on an SSD;
- keep additional space available for projects, caches, JDKs, plugins, and build files;
- close unnecessary memory-intensive applications while working; and
- maintain a stable internet connection for downloads and updates.

## Installation options

### Option A — JetBrains Toolbox App

JetBrains Toolbox App is convenient when using multiple JetBrains products or managing frequent updates.

1. Download [JetBrains Toolbox App](https://www.jetbrains.com/toolbox-app/).
2. Install and open Toolbox.
3. Locate IntelliJ IDEA.
4. Select the current stable version.
5. Start the installation.
6. Launch IntelliJ IDEA from Toolbox when installation finishes.

Toolbox can manage IDE updates and installed versions from one interface.

### Option B — Standalone installer

1. Open the [IntelliJ IDEA download page](https://www.jetbrains.com/idea/download/).
2. Confirm that the detected operating system is correct.
3. Download the appropriate installer.
4. Run the downloaded installer.
5. Review every installation option before continuing.
6. Finish the installation and launch IntelliJ IDEA.

## Windows installation options

Depending on the installer version, Windows may offer options such as:

- creating a desktop shortcut;
- adding the IntelliJ launcher directory to PATH;
- adding an **Open Folder as Project** context-menu action; and
- associating supported project-file types with IntelliJ IDEA.

Select only the options you understand and need. Adding the launcher to PATH is useful for opening projects from a terminal but is not required for the first lesson.

Restart Windows only if the installer requests it or a selected integration is not immediately available.

## macOS installation

1. Download the macOS installer that matches the computer processor.
2. Open the downloaded disk image.
3. Drag IntelliJ IDEA into the Applications folder.
4. Open IntelliJ IDEA from Applications.
5. Approve the macOS security prompt if it appears.

On Apple Silicon, use the ARM64 build. On an Intel Mac, use the Intel build.

## Linux installation

JetBrains supports installation through its Toolbox App and downloadable archive. Package-manager availability varies by distribution.

When using the archive:

1. Download the Linux archive from JetBrains.
2. Extract it into an appropriate applications directory.
3. Open a terminal in the extracted directory.
4. Launch the IDE using the provided script in the bin directory.
5. Optionally create a desktop entry from IntelliJ IDEA after startup.

Follow the exact commands in the current [JetBrains Linux installation instructions](https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/installation-guide.html#standalone) rather than copying commands intended for a different distribution or release.

## First launch

During the first launch, IntelliJ IDEA may ask whether to import settings.

Choose **Do not import settings** when:

- this is the first IntelliJ installation;
- no previous configuration should be reused; or
- a clean learning environment is preferred.

Import settings only when a known, compatible configuration already exists.

The IDE may also display:

- a privacy or data-sharing choice;
- license information for paid features;
- theme and appearance settings;
- available plugins; and
- a welcome screen with options such as **New Project**, **Open**, and **Get from VCS**.

Read each screen instead of accepting options automatically.

## Initial configuration

### Theme

Choose a light or dark theme according to comfort and accessibility needs. The theme does not affect program behavior and can be changed later in Settings.

### Updates

Install stable updates when practical. Read release information before updating during an important project, and allow IntelliJ IDEA to restart if required.

### Plugins

Begin with the built-in functionality. Install an additional plugin only when a lesson or project requires it.

Before installing a plugin:

1. verify its publisher;
2. check compatibility with the installed IntelliJ version;
3. read the permissions and description;
4. review recent maintenance activity; and
5. restart the IDE if requested.

Too many plugins can increase startup time, memory use, and the chance of compatibility problems.

## Why IntelliJ IDEA uses memory

IntelliJ IDEA performs continuous background work, including:

- indexing project files;
- analyzing code;
- detecting errors;
- calculating code-completion suggestions;
- tracking version-control changes; and
- running installed plugins.

Temporary higher CPU or memory usage is normal while a project is being indexed. Avoid closing the IDE during initial indexing unless it becomes unresponsive for an extended period.

## Installation verification

After installation, verify the following:

- [ ] IntelliJ IDEA starts without an installation error.
- [ ] The Welcome screen appears.
- [ ] **New Project**, **Open**, and **Get from VCS** are available.
- [ ] Settings can be opened.
- [ ] The Plugins page loads.
- [ ] The installed IntelliJ IDEA version can be found under **Help → About**.
- [ ] The selected edition or license status is understood.
- [ ] At least 10 GB of working disk space remains available.

Record the installed version and operating system in your study notes.

## Common installation problems

### The installer does not open

- Confirm that the installer matches the operating system and processor.
- Download it again from the official JetBrains website.
- Check whether the operating system blocked the file.
- Confirm that the current account has permission to install applications.

### IntelliJ IDEA starts slowly

- Allow initial indexing to finish.
- Close unnecessary applications.
- Disable unneeded third-party plugins.
- Confirm that sufficient RAM and disk space are available.
- Prefer SSD storage when possible.

### The IDE does not start

- Restart the computer.
- Launch the IDE without opening a large project.
- Check the official JetBrains troubleshooting documentation.
- Record any exact error message before requesting help.

### A paid-feature notice appears

The unified product can expose features that require an Ultimate subscription. Core Java and Kotlin learning features remain available without purchasing a subscription. Review the feature notice and continue with free functionality when appropriate.

## Completion criteria

This installation stage is complete when IntelliJ IDEA launches successfully, the installed version is recorded, and the learner can reach the Welcome screen.

The next stage will configure the JDK and verify that IntelliJ IDEA can create and run a JVM project.

